            <ArticleTitle>Influence of fatty acids on mitochondrial metabolism of adipocyte progenitors and endothelial cells.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>128-34</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/13813455.2012.668193</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">In obesity, the cells are exposed to excessive amounts of nutrients, especially free fatty acids (FFAs) that induce a variety of metabolic changes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We investigated the effect of FFAs on the mitochondrial function in different cell populations under stress conditions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Human adipose tissue progenitor cells (SVF) or endothelial cells (HUVECs) were incubated with 30μM of selected saturated or unsaturated FFA for 24 h, at times supplemented with 5ng/mL tumour necrosis factor alpha (TNFα) for the last 4 h. Changes in oxygen respiration rate, mitochondrial membrane potential (mitoMP) and total ATP content were monitored.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Saturated palmitic acid demonstrated no effect, while a selection of unsaturated FFAs ameliorated metabolism of the progenitor SVF cells. TNFα either did not affect or nullified some of the favourable FFA-induced effects.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The mitoMP was the most sensitive parameter reflecting positive impact of the unsaturated FFA on the adipose SVF cells' metabolism.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
